WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.920
- California gets earthquakes,

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.330
which means that the building codes here

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.070
have some pretty strict requirements.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.320
There's a lot you can do
with computer simulation,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.760
but at some point,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.760
new building techniques have
to be tested in the real world.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.100
The massive 10-storey building behind me

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.010
has been constructed to test
and certify a new design,

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.680
a "post-tensioned rocking wall".

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.040
Basically, there's a massive,
heavy wood and steel spine

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.380
that runs all the way up
from the floor to the roof,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.090
bracing and dampening the whole thing,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.620
which, along with new fireproofing,

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.040
might allow wooden buildings to be
far taller than ever before.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.410
Does it work?
Well, to really test it,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.810
you need an earthquake,
so we're about to get one.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.970
The building is sitting
on the Shake Table here

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.130
at the University of California San Diego.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.820
This whole site is locked down
because that table is ready to go.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.140
There's about to be a test.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.370
But I was here a few months back

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.170
while that building was
still under construction,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.030
and one of the folks in charge
showed me around

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.830
while everything was powered down.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.390
We started in the equipment building,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.670
where there's colossal
accumulators and pumps

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.120
that pressurize hydraulic fluid
to 5,000 psi,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.760
which is more than a hundred times
the pressure of your average car tire.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.067
This is really big engineering.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.290
So how much can the shake table move?

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.740
- If you're going along the
long axis, the x direction,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.930
we can go plus/minus 35 inches.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.630
Going in the lateral, the y-axis,

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.910
we can go plus/minus 15 inches.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.520
And in the z, up and down,
it's plus/minus five.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.940
We have the largest
payload capacity in the world.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.710
We can move 2,000 tons.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.350
So about five million pounds of mass.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.580
This 10-story building is
about 700,000 pounds total.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.664
So that's maybe 10%, 15% of our capacity.

00:01:36.664 --> 00:01:37.497
- Wow.
- Yeah.

00:01:37.673 --> 00:01:40.140
- They're going to shake a
10-storey building back and forth,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.700
and it's not even a big deal for them.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.560
Also, it's not just
up, down, and sideways.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.960
The shake table also does roll,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.041
pitch,

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:47.858
and yaw

00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:49.710
When it was originally built,
about 20 years ago,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.690
it did only go back and forth
in one direction.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:52.590
They can still do that.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.810
They can shake in precise
mathematical sine waves

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.970
if they want to for a
particular experiment.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.330
But these days, they can
also replay actual records

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.130
of real-life earthquakes.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.729
Although, that turns out to be
far more complicated than I thought.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:06.900
- Before we build anything on it,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.030
we need to do some tuning
for the control system.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.100
So bare table, when there's
nothing on the table,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.360
we do some tuning.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.980
Then, when you put the specimen on,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.680
those dynamics, the specimen
dynamics actually affect

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.000
the control loop.
- Right.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.340
- So we need to do some further tuning

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.860
and actually filter out
some of those frequencies

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.500
in order to successfully
move the shake table.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.170
- Oh, because if the
building that you've made

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.930
has a resonant frequency...

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.610
- It interferes with the control system.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.620
- Right.
- So we need to filter out

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.270
those resonant frequencies.
- Oh.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.819
So you're not just playing
back an earthquake.

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:42.660
You're trying to get as
close to that as possible

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.313
while dealing with whatever the building
wants to do as you shake it?

00:02:46.313 --> 00:02:47.880
- Right, if you're doing multiple shakes.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.410
So the first few tests,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.660
the building hasn't
been altered in any way.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.580
- Then the building might shift or break

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.620
or you might change-
- You might change something.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.453
- Right.

00:02:56.453 --> 00:02:58.680
- And that might affect the controls.

00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:00.480
- Next up on the tour was the connector

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.670
between the power building
and the shake table,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.760
where again, there is stuff
that feels like over-engineering

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.161
until you realize just how much
mass this thing has to move.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:10.500
- Everything here is color coded,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.380
so the red is high pressure
and blue is return.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.960
These large pipes here,
they're 12-inch diameter,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.490
they're like over an inch thick.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.220
Inside this pipe is 3,000 psi pressure.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.310
- I feel like every curve in that pipe

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.470
was something that had to be
really carefully engineered,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.680
'cause every time 3,000 psi
fluid has to change direction,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.090
you're going to get
water-hammer effects.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:30.990
- Oh, absolutely.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.850
- The hydraulic shock,
right, that's the term,

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.120
it will add vibrations
to the table itself?

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.390
- Exactly, it'll cause a lot of headache,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.460
not to mention the damage that it can do

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.110
if one of these connections breaks.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:44.220
- Yeah.
- Then you're getting,

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.070
you know, 3,000 psi oil
shooting everywhere.

00:03:47.211 --> 00:03:49.680
- And that's why the whole
area is locked off right now.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.640
Everything is charged up.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.220
Warning lights are flashing,
a test is about to happen.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.960
It's very unlikely
there'll be anything wrong.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:55.950
This is not the first test.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.230
They started small a few weeks ago.
They're working up.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.120
The safety margin is massive.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.430
But right now, inside and under
those buildings

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.770
is not technically a
safe place for humans,

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.209
just in case.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.447
So from here, 5,000 psi
in the accumulators,

00:04:09.447 --> 00:04:11.940
3,000 psi running through these pipes

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.860
at who knows what speed.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:14.970
- Right.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.341
And they come over here and
make a U-turn!

00:04:17.341 --> 00:04:19.980
- Agh! It does a U-turn!

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.898
- Yeah, this is why we
have a damper system here

00:04:22.898 --> 00:04:25.080
is to make sure...
- Good grief.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.520
- ...there's no vibrations here.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.980
So this was very highly engineered

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.840
to make sure everything's okay.
- Yeah, it was!

00:04:30.963 --> 00:04:33.390
Don't ask me exactly why
there's a U-turn in the pipes.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.370
It's to do with retrofitting
new improvements

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.080
around existing concrete structures.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.840
But I do know that no sane engineer

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.700
would put a U-turn in that pipe

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.920
unless they absolutely had to.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.230
- Now we're actually
under the shake table.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.850
So this is the shake table platen.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.610
The 10-storey building is
currently sitting on it.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:51.960
This is what we move.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.820
Everything you see on
this side is mirrored.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.560
So right now we're on the south side.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.300
We have six vertical actuators.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.710
So there's three of them on this side,

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.210
three of them on the north side.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:03.180
The vertical actuators do
the up and down movement

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.880
and then the roll and the pitch movements.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:06.840
- Okay, oh yeah.
- Right, so you can-

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.060
- 'Cause you can actuate
each one a different amount

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.260
and that will do-
- Right, you can lift one

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.460
and drop the other.
- Yeah.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.590
- And they're not actually
attached to the shake table.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.660
So this thing is a glide plate.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.040
The vertical actuator actually has
a 32-inch diameter circular surface.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.470
And that creates like a vacuum chamber
between the glide plate and the surface,

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.830
very low friction.
That vacuum gets filled with oil.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.140
So when we push the actuator up,

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.820
that table gets lifted up with it,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.920
but it can still freely slide around.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.360
What we definitely don't want

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.676
is for that seal to ever open.

00:05:38.676 --> 00:05:39.628
Right?

00:05:39.628 --> 00:05:43.110
- Okay, yeah, absolutely.
- You don't want the actuator to be unseated

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.342
from that glide plate.
- We're laughing,

00:05:44.342 --> 00:05:45.175
but what would be catastrophic.

00:05:45.175 --> 00:05:46.577
Okay.
- That would be catastrophic, right.

00:05:46.753 --> 00:05:48.060
-So last stop on the tour,

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.860
we've seen how it moves up and down.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.570
Next is the side to side.

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.510
- Right over our heads are
our horizontal actuators.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:55.641
So there are two-
- I hadn't looked up.

00:05:55.641 --> 00:05:57.590
- There's two horizontal actuators-
- Wow.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.270
- In a V shape on either side,

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.200
and they're the ones

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.646
that allow the x and y
and that yaw motion.

00:06:05.646 --> 00:06:08.430
- These are...?
- These are what we call hold-down struts.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.860
They're passive actuators, if you will.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.340
They're basically a piston with
nitrogen filled on one side.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.250
- Right.
- Constantly applying a downward force.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.700
So we have three of them.
There's a couple of reasons for it.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:24.900
One is, remember when I said we don't want
those vertical actuators to ever unseat?

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.910
- Right.
- So you're always pulling down.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.620
- Okay.
- You're never going to unseat those.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.230
And the other one would be
for the overturning moment

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.420
of the shake table.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.010
We have a 10-storey building on it.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.230
If it wants to tip over, it's
going to want to lift and-

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.220
- So it's always-
- This stops that.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.630
- Getting pulled down for safety?

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.176
- Always a restoring force, yeah.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:42.660
- I want to just make that clear.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.970
They're constantly
pulling down on the table

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.350
because that building on top

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:47.790
can have so much inertia

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:49.830
that it might just want
to keep tipping over.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.541
So it's got to fail safe.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.400
The amount of safety systems here,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.208
if anything trips, presumably
everything just shuts down

00:06:56.208 --> 00:06:57.569
as quickly as it can.
- Everything shuts down,

00:06:57.569 --> 00:06:58.710
but it's a controlled shutdown.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.637
You can't leave it off-axis
and you can't just be like,

00:07:00.637 --> 00:07:03.060
"Okay, return to zero immediately,"

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:03.893
because you-
- Right.

00:07:03.893 --> 00:07:05.610
- You might actually do more damage

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.110
on that reversal.
- Yeah.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.020
- It has to be a controlled, slow ramp down

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.559
to bring it back to its zero position.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:12.533
- So here we go,

00:07:12.533 --> 00:07:14.190
the area around the building
has been cleared.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.370
The scientists in the control room
are going through the startup sequence.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.870
This is just one test in a hundred,

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.730
another routine earthquake!

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.230
And I don't mean to
undercut the drama here,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:25.140
but the point is that the
building will survive.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.740
This is not testing to destruction.
They're not even going to 100% power.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.789
This is just another day
for the Shake Table.

00:07:31.789 --> 00:07:36.789
- "Commencing in five, four, three, two, one..."

00:07:54.662 --> 00:07:58.415
- I know it's fine, but I do want
to turn and run.

00:07:58.415 --> 00:08:00.854
I absolutely
want to turn and run.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:06.133
Wow.

00:08:09.439 --> 00:08:11.399
- "Next test will commence in 10 minutes."

00:08:12.382 --> 00:08:13.849
- All right.

